From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:59:28 +0200
Subject: watchdog: add a driver for the Intel GRX500 GIC watchdog

The GRX500 has no watchdog block. What the vendor BSP presents as one is
the MIPS GIC's architectural per-VP watchdog plus a single SoC register,
RCU_IAP_WDT_RST_EN, without which an expiry raises a VP-local interrupt
and goes no further. This driver is its only writer.

The 4.9 BSP carries two drivers for this hardware and it matters which
one is read. drivers/watchdog/grx500_wdt.c is the one that ships --
defconfig:2251 CONFIG_GRX500_IAP_WDT=y, fed through .avm_hw_ping by
avm_new/ar7wdt_hw_shim.c:481 -- while drivers/char/avm_new/grx_wdt.c does
not build at all, because avm_new/Kconfig gates AVM_WATCHDOG_GRX500 on
!AVM_WATCHDOG_SHIM and defconfig:1821 turns the shim on.

No per-CPU machinery. Both vendor drivers spread the watchdog across
every CPU via smp_call_function_single(). This platform runs UP --
"nosmp" is on every command line -- so the driver owns the boot VP's
watchdog and reaches it through the plain VP-local register window.

Feeding is the vendor's read-modify-write, and that is the point rather
than an accident. WDINTR and WDRESET live in GIC_Vx_WD_CONFIG0 and
neither tree states whether they are write-1-to-clear or plain
read/write. An absolute write of a value carrying WDINTR would clear the
bit under one reading and assert it under the other; "read, OR in START,
write back" cannot turn WDINTR from clear to set under either, which is
what makes it safe on a recurring path. The single absolute write that
does set both bits is at probe, once, to drop stale status, and it is
ordered before the interrupt is unmasked.

Stale status is retired at probe by a write, a read and a conditional
second write, in that order, and the read in the middle is the point.
The two vendor drivers clear WDINTR by mutually exclusive means and only
one of them can be doing anything: gic_wd_setup_on() writes the bits
back as 1s, which clears them if they are write-1-to-clear and sets
WDINTR if they are plain read/write, while grx_wdt.c writes CONFIG0 = 0
and does the opposite. No ordering of the two clears the bit under both
readings, and putting the zero last would destroy the evidence, since
both readings then read back 0. Writing the 1s first and clearing only
if WDINTR survived leaves the bit clear either way and makes the
intervening read a verdict on which silicon this is -- on every boot,
because a stale bit cannot change either branch.

That verdict is worth having beyond tidiness. If WDINTR were left set,
the expiry interrupt would stay masked for the whole of the boot after a
watchdog reset -- the post-mortem boot -- and every keepalive
read-modify-write would write the set bit back, so in SCD mode every
expiry would count as a second expiry and the box would reset after one
window rather than two. Safe, because max_hw_heartbeat_ms already
assumes one window, but not something to do by accident.

The counter rate is measured, not assumed, and the device tree's clock
phandle is not taken on trust: the vendor hands the watchdog the CPU
clock but arms at 0xffffffff
and re-arms every second, so a factor of two in the rate is invisible in
its code and was never validated by it -- and mti,gic-timer, which would
have pinned the GIC block's rate down independently, is not built on this
platform. Probe therefore free-runs the countdown for 10 ms against CP0
count, which is mips_hpt_frequency and drives system timekeeping, and
derives ticks per second from the ratio.

The three ways that measurement can fail are reported separately, because
one of them is not a measurement problem. A counter that never
decremented means this watchdog can never bite, which is the single
outcome that looks healthy from userspace right up to the moment it is
needed, so it fails probe rather than registering a device that procd
will happily feed forever.

Guessing high is the expensive direction, which also sets the window
policy: max_hw_heartbeat_ms is one full countdown less a margin, never
the two that SCD's second-expiry reset would allow. Assuming 2x when it
is 1x makes the hardware bite before the core's virtual deadline, which
is a reset loop; assuming 1x when it is 2x only resets late.

The userspace timeout is bridged by the watchdog core. The window is a
handful of seconds while procd asks for 30. Setting max_hw_heartbeat_ms
and leaving max_timeout at zero is what lets the core accept the
WDIOC_SETTIMEOUT and run its own keepalive worker at half the window.
procd discards that ioctl's return value and never recomputes its 5 s
feed interval, so a driver that clamped and reported back would be
indistinguishable from one that accepted -- right up to the reset loop.
The vendor driver clamps.

The expiry interrupt is optional and wired to the pretimeout governors.
It is a GIC local interrupt, so a percpu request, and on a board with no
JTAG it is the only chance to see where the CPU was stuck. It cannot
acknowledge the expiry: clearing WDINTR cancels the reset that follows
it, which would leave a watchdog that warns and never bites. No vendor
code clears it from a handler either -- the shipping one only warns, the
unbuilt one masks -- and disable_percpu_irq() is that mask, needed
because GIC local interrupts are level triggered and
gic_local_irq_controller has no ->irq_eoi.

Masking is not permanent, because surviving the first expiry is the
designed default: the core's keepalive re-arms seconds later, and a
stall long enough to trip the watchdog but short enough to recover --
a NAND erase, say -- would otherwise cost the pretimeout report for the
rest of the boot. Each keepalive re-reads CONFIG0 and unmasks only once
WDINTR has gone clear. That read also answers, at runtime and once,
which semantics the status bits actually have, which is more than either
vendor tree records.

WDIOF_PRETIMEOUT is set because watchdog_have_pretimeout() is exactly
that flag, and without it wdt_is_visible() hides the pretimeout_governor
knob and the governors can be built with no way to select one at runtime.

Probe arms the watchdog and declares WDOG_HW_RUNNING, so the boot is
covered rather than only the part after procd opens the device. The
vendor does the same, about a second into init. It is safe because
CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED makes the core start feeding at
registration and CONFIG_WATCHDOG_OPEN_TIMEOUT is 0, so a late or absent
procd can never make it stop.

Builtin only: the CPS accessors resolve mips_gic_base, which is not
exported.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

